Now we put it in like bacteria
or tumor cells in cell lines

00:32:21.360 --> 00:32:22.840
or mice or something,

00:32:22.840 --> 00:32:26.720
so that we can follow these
green light-up proteins

00:32:26.720 --> 00:32:28.083
or whole cells.

00:32:32.275 --> 00:32:34.240
- [Teacher] When did you know

00:32:34.240 --> 00:32:36.730
that you wanted to pursue science?

00:32:36.730 --> 00:32:38.210
Was it sometime in high school?

00:32:38.210 --> 00:32:41.133
Was it during your undergraduate career?

00:32:42.380 --> 00:32:44.570
- Yeah, awesome question.

00:32:44.570 --> 00:32:49.260
So in high school, I knew
I really liked science.

00:32:49.260 --> 00:32:52.990
I had an awesome living
environment teacher

00:32:52.990 --> 00:32:56.560
who like told all of us
we could be scientists.

00:32:56.560 --> 00:32:57.710
I'm like, Oh, that's super cool.

00:32:57.710 --> 00:33:00.400
But like, people like me
don't become scientists.

00:33:00.400 --> 00:33:02.120
We're just like normal people.

00:33:02.120 --> 00:33:07.120
So I wanted to be like an
elementary school science teacher

00:33:08.800 --> 00:33:10.970
because I like teaching,

00:33:10.970 --> 00:33:13.960
but I still wanted to learn about science.

00:33:13.960 --> 00:33:16.100
So I went to undergrad.

00:33:16.100 --> 00:33:17.700
Stony Brook was super good

00:33:17.700 --> 00:33:20.830
about like getting everyone
involved in research.

00:33:20.830 --> 00:33:23.960
So they're like, if you
want to try it, join a lab,

00:33:23.960 --> 00:33:25.003
see what happens.

00:33:26.350 --> 00:33:28.600
So I did, I was like,

00:33:28.600 --> 00:33:32.040
Oh, I'll probably just
keep doing teaching things,

00:33:32.040 --> 00:33:34.720
but might as well see
what research is like.

00:33:34.720 --> 00:33:38.580
And then like I did, I worked in a lab,

00:33:38.580 --> 00:33:41.652
I got to play with cells
and do experiments.

00:33:41.652 --> 00:33:42.920
I was like, okay, cool.

00:33:42.920 --> 00:33:44.640
Now I want to do this.

00:33:44.640 --> 00:33:48.520
I didn't realize like we
could be part of science.

00:33:48.520 --> 00:33:50.770
I thought I could just
like, watch it happen.

00:33:51.810 --> 00:33:53.770
So I've always loved science,

00:33:53.770 --> 00:33:55.880
but I didn't realize I could do it

00:33:55.880 --> 00:33:57.070
until I got to undergrad.

00:33:57.070 --> 00:33:59.043
So yeah.

00:34:00.690 --> 00:34:02.490
- [Teacher] Thank you.

00:34:02.490 --> 00:34:04.290
- Of course, yeah.

00:34:04.290 --> 00:34:05.960
There's a question in the chat

00:34:05.960 --> 00:34:09.963
about how my tuberculosis
research is going.

00:34:11.650 --> 00:34:13.052
It's slow.

00:34:13.052 --> 00:34:17.263
TB, like the E. coli we
were working with before,

00:34:19.050 --> 00:34:22.230
doubles its population every 30 minutes.

00:34:22.230 --> 00:34:24.610
So it's really fast.

00:34:24.610 --> 00:34:27.933
TB doubles every 24 hours.

00:34:27.933 --> 00:34:30.500
So it's really slow,

00:34:30.500 --> 00:34:34.390
but I'm looking at genes
that are super important

00:34:34.390 --> 00:34:37.500
in drug-resistant tuberculosis.

00:34:37.500 --> 00:34:41.220
So when these cells
naturally can't be killed

00:34:41.220 --> 00:34:42.570
by antibiotics,

00:34:42.570 --> 00:34:47.310
what in their DNA is
letting them survive still?

00:34:47.310 --> 00:34:51.290
So like, do they grow really slowly

00:34:51.290 --> 00:34:53.740
so that the antibiotics can't get in?

00:34:53.740 --> 00:34:57.260
Do they have like a
really thick cell wall?

00:34:57.260 --> 00:35:02.260
So that even if there is
like stress inside the cell,

00:35:03.750 --> 00:35:05.200
nothing from outside gets in.

00:35:06.434 --> 00:35:10.260
I still have a lot to do,
but I'm really enjoying it.

00:35:10.260 --> 00:35:11.433
Super fun.

00:35:13.040 --> 00:35:13.873
Yeah.

00:35:15.030 --> 00:35:17.605
So someone has asked about advice

00:35:17.605 --> 00:35:20.840
for those who want to be in my place.

00:35:20.840 --> 00:35:22.120
So yeah if you want

00:35:22.120 --> 00:35:26.273
to be a science researcher, any capacity,

00:35:29.897 --> 00:35:33.163
definitely like the first
step is to do undergrad,

00:35:34.510 --> 00:35:36.320
probably in a science.

00:35:36.320 --> 00:35:38.690
If you have the opportunity at that school

00:35:38.690 --> 00:35:40.890
to do undergraduate research,

00:35:40.890 --> 00:35:42.970
I super recommend it,

00:35:42.970 --> 00:35:45.550
both from like an
application standpoint, like,

00:35:45.550 --> 00:35:48.270
Oh, grad schools love to
see undergrad research.

00:35:48.270 --> 00:35:51.530
But also from like, it's super cool.

00:35:51.530 --> 00:35:53.370
You get to try all these new things.

00:35:53.370 --> 00:35:55.410
And people, like grad students like us

00:35:55.410 --> 00:35:58.623
are super excited to
have students come in,

00:35:59.580 --> 00:36:02.485
young scholars like yourselves
who want to learn things,

00:36:02.485 --> 00:36:04.540
who want to try stuff out in the lab.

00:36:04.540 --> 00:36:08.003
And then as far as like advice goes,

00:36:09.220 --> 00:36:12.383
one, know that you belong here.

00:36:14.180 --> 00:36:15.860
You're super smart.

00:36:15.860 --> 00:36:19.350
You're coming this far.

00:36:19.350 --> 00:36:20.370
We need you.

00:36:20.370 --> 00:36:23.680
We need young, smart scientists like you,

00:36:23.680 --> 00:36:26.740
and all of the like different ideas

00:36:26.740 --> 00:36:29.230
and perspectives you bring
from your backgrounds.

00:36:29.230 --> 00:36:31.139
We want that.

00:36:31.139 --> 00:36:36.060
And that not only do you belong here,

00:36:36.060 --> 00:36:38.740
but like just because
something goes wrong,

00:36:38.740 --> 00:36:42.100
you don't need to,
like, you can't give up.

00:36:42.100 --> 00:36:44.950
The biggest thing is like keeping going

00:36:44.950 --> 00:36:46.730
even when you get negative results,

00:36:46.730 --> 00:36:50.200
or nothing grows on your plates.

00:36:50.200 --> 00:36:53.690
So, I'm gonna let y'all
in on a little secret.

00:36:53.690 --> 00:36:57.920
I did transformations of genes
that I was interested in.

00:36:57.920 --> 00:37:00.240
'Cause like I have to
do it for my research.

00:37:00.240 --> 00:37:03.853
At the same time as I was
making these for y'all,

00:37:04.944 --> 00:37:08.530
and some of them work really nicely.

00:37:08.530 --> 00:37:11.793
Like I got those little
colonies, little dots.

00:37:13.070 --> 00:37:14.702
Some of them didn't.

00:37:14.702 --> 00:37:17.300
There is nothing growing on this plate,

00:37:17.300 --> 00:37:19.740
and there shouldn't be
things growing on it.

00:37:19.740 --> 00:37:24.690
So a lot of science is like,
Oh man, I gotta do that again.

00:37:24.690 --> 00:37:26.610
Or what did I do wrong?

00:37:26.610 --> 00:37:28.390
Did I forget to add something?

00:37:28.390 --> 00:37:30.120
Or is this just like not working

00:37:30.120 --> 00:37:33.543
because the science is
it's a negative result.

00:37:34.900 --> 00:37:37.110
So yeah, you just got to keep going

00:37:37.110 --> 00:37:39.403
and really think about what you're doing.

00:37:43.860 --> 00:37:47.160
And also, I'm going to
give y'all my email address

00:37:47.160 --> 00:37:47.993
at the end.

00:37:47.993 --> 00:37:51.600
So if you want to chat
more about like grad school

00:37:51.600 --> 00:37:54.970
or undergrad, or even
like high school things,

00:37:54.970 --> 00:37:58.310
I'm happy to talk about
all this kind of stuff.

00:37:58.310 --> 00:38:01.090
And I can do it for a really long time.

00:38:01.090 --> 00:38:05.713
Someone asked about any other
cool science, art ideas.

00:38:07.300 --> 00:38:10.480
So since I'm a microbiologist,

00:38:10.480 --> 00:38:12.140
I'm super partial to all

00:38:12.140 --> 00:38:17.140
of the different like micro
painting with bacteria things.

00:38:18.270 --> 00:38:22.310
But there are a lot of
like small groups that...

00:38:23.640 --> 00:38:26.260
So I mentioned that I'm vegan, right?

00:38:26.260 --> 00:38:29.550
So there is a whole
company out in California,

00:38:29.550 --> 00:38:31.070
of course it's California,

00:38:31.070 --> 00:38:36.070
making vegan cheese by having
E. coli make the milk protein.

00:38:37.980 --> 00:38:42.980
So they cloned that whey
gene from cow's milk

00:38:43.540 --> 00:38:46.540
into E. coli, and they
produce it like that.

00:38:46.540 --> 00:38:50.413
So you could have legit vegan cow's milk.

00:38:51.780 --> 00:38:52.613
You don't have to worry

00:38:52.613 --> 00:38:54.790
about like the gross vegan cheese problem

00:38:54.790 --> 00:38:59.030
where like it doesn't melt, or
it doesn't taste like cheese.

00:38:59.030 --> 00:39:02.513
It's fully vegan, cruelty-free cows milk.

00:39:03.480 --> 00:39:06.700
Same with like in vitro meats.

00:39:06.700 --> 00:39:10.760
Like, Oh, what if we can make
a hamburger in a Petri dish?

00:39:10.760 --> 00:39:12.310
I would eat that.

00:39:12.310 --> 00:39:13.170
I'm not vegan

00:39:13.170 --> 00:39:15.920
because I don't like the
way hamburgers taste.

00:39:15.920 --> 00:39:16.843
They're very good.

00:39:18.533 --> 00:39:20.120
As far as art goes,

00:39:20.120 --> 00:39:25.120
there are people who make
like bioluminescent plants.

00:39:25.130 --> 00:39:27.900
So plants that grow different colors,

00:39:27.900 --> 00:39:31.678
and they have all these cool,
like little miniature gardens

00:39:31.678 --> 00:39:33.973
with different light-up leaves.

00:39:36.390 --> 00:39:38.800
I'm not super familiar
with the other ones,

00:39:38.800 --> 00:39:43.220
but I think if you like
Googled biology art,

00:39:43.220 --> 00:39:47.117
or something like that, you
could find really cool things.

00:39:47.117 --> 00:39:50.950
There's this international genetically

00:39:50.950 --> 00:39:54.400
engineered machine competition, iGEM,

00:39:54.400 --> 00:39:57.450
that has high schoolers and
undergrads and grad students

00:39:57.450 --> 00:40:01.000
do projects, and there's a
whole set of like tracks,

00:40:01.000 --> 00:40:04.400
for people who just want
to make art with biology.

00:40:04.400 --> 00:40:06.620
So they have some, yeah.

00:40:06.620 --> 00:40:08.310
Oh Jeanne, you wanted to say something?

00:40:08.310 --> 00:40:09.371
- [Jeanne] Yeah.

00:40:09.371 --> 00:40:12.540
I'll share that the American
Society for Microbiology

00:40:12.540 --> 00:40:14.880
has an art competition every year.

00:40:14.880 --> 00:40:18.020
And if anybody has ever done urban barcode

00:40:18.020 --> 00:40:20.120
or now BioBus,

00:40:20.120 --> 00:40:22.150
Christine Marazzi, if anybody knows her,

00:40:22.150 --> 00:40:23.710
she wins it all the time.

00:40:23.710 --> 00:40:26.750
She (indistinct) New York city skylines

00:40:26.750 --> 00:40:28.950
in different fluorescent colors,

00:40:28.950 --> 00:40:31.420
like on big giant dishes of agar.

00:40:31.420 --> 00:40:32.350
It's really cool.

00:40:32.350 --> 00:40:33.860
You should check it out.

00:40:33.860 --> 00:40:37.660
And I think there's another
company called Gingko Bioworks.

00:40:37.660 --> 00:40:39.900
It's a Massachusetts-based startup,

00:40:39.900 --> 00:40:43.830
that basically genetically
engineers whatever they can.

00:40:43.830 --> 00:40:47.450
Like if you go to like a candle store,

00:40:47.450 --> 00:40:49.840
or someone you know is
like selling candles

00:40:49.840 --> 00:40:52.190
and those candles smell like rose,

00:40:52.190 --> 00:40:55.930
a lot of times that rose odor,

00:40:55.930 --> 00:41:00.220
it's made through
genetically-engineered microbes.

00:41:00.220 --> 00:41:01.220
They just pump it out,

00:41:01.220 --> 00:41:03.370
and then these companies buy these odors

00:41:03.370 --> 00:41:06.920
and pop it into their
lotions and their candles.

00:41:06.920 --> 00:41:11.911
It's one of the ways that we
get that smelliness, I suppose,

00:41:11.911 --> 00:41:14.363
of our common products.

00:41:17.340 --> 00:41:18.173
- Thank you.

00:41:18.173 --> 00:41:19.113
I totally didn't know that.

00:41:20.370 --> 00:41:21.203
- [Jeanne] It's fun.

00:41:21.203 --> 00:41:23.390
I love thinking about the applications

00:41:23.390 --> 00:41:24.910
of genetic engineering.

00:41:24.910 --> 00:41:29.910
I feel like you can
basically dream up anything

00:41:30.210 --> 00:41:31.670
and then make anything.

00:41:31.670 --> 00:41:33.323
It's so cool, you know.

